<article class="env-lemma" id="016K">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/016K">Lemma <span data-tag="016K">14.5.2</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Given a cosimplicial object $U$ in $\mathcal{C}$ we obtain a sequence of objects $U_ n = U([n])$ endowed with the morphisms $\delta ^ n_ j = U(\delta ^ n_ j) : U_{n - 1} \to U_ n$ and $\sigma ^ n_ j = U(\sigma ^ n_ j) : U_{n + 1} \to U_ n$. These morphisms satisfy the relations displayed in Lemma <a href="/tag/0167" data-tag="0167">14.2.3</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Conversely, given a sequence of objects $U_ n$ and morphisms $\delta ^ n_ j$, $\sigma ^ n_ j$ satisfying these relations there exists a unique cosimplicial object $U$ in $\mathcal{C}$ such that $U_ n = U([n])$, $\delta ^ n_ j = U(\delta ^ n_ j)$, and $\sigma ^ n_ j = U(\sigma ^ n_ j)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A morphism between cosimplicial objects $U$ and $U'$ is given by a family of morphisms $U_ n \to U'_ n$ commuting with the morphisms $\delta ^ n_ j$ and $\sigma ^ n_ j$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0168" data-tag="0168">14.2.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>